#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script --quiet
"""Prints the usage of each account that the loop builds with, and advice on whether the loop may start more work.

Each account's line gives its meters, and the usage per agent-minute of each meter that its advice counts with. It ends
with the account's advice, which names the agent types that the account can start:

  go         Every type fits.
  <types>    Only the types listed fit, such as builder-small, researcher, reviewer.
  stop       No type fits. Start nothing on the account until its running agents end or a meter resets.
  parked     A weekly meter reached 99% more than 10 hours before its reset. Start nothing on the account until that
             reset.

The types of a Claude account are the agent types in .claude/agents, and a Codex account has the type codex. A type
fits while each of the account's meters, 5-hour and weekly, has room for a long run of the type, after what the agents
that run on the account still need:

- A meter's room is what is left of it, up to 100% for the 5-hour meter and 99% for a weekly meter.
- A type's run is the usage of the 90th percentile of the minutes of the type's earlier runs in .loop/agents.jsonl, at
  the account's usage per agent-minute. A type without an earlier run takes the runs of all types.
- A running agent still needs the usage of the 90th percentile of how much longer the earlier runs of its type went
  on, among those that ran at least as long as it has. On the orchestrator's own account, the orchestrator still needs
  its usage until the weekly reset, at the share of its time that it worked so far.
- Only the usage before a meter's reset counts, because the room comes back then.

The usage per agent-minute is the rise of a meter since the newest reading of the account that is {period} to {gap}
minutes old, over the minutes that the account's agents worked since that reading. On its own account, the
orchestrator counts as an agent for the minutes that its transcript shows it working. When the agents didn't work or
the meter didn't rise, the meter takes the last usage per agent-minute that it measured, and before it measured one,
the usage that scripts/loop/account_room.py starts with.

The line after the accounts names the Claude account to start each agent type on, other than the auditor: the account
with the lowest 5-hour meter among those where the type fits. Between accounts with the same 5-hour meter, it names the
one whose week resets first, and then one other than the orchestrator's own account.

With --start-on TYPE, the meter reads no account, and prints the Claude account to start an agent of the type on, as
that line names it, or "no account". It takes each account's newest reading in .loop/meter.jsonl, and leaves out an
account whose newest reading is more than {start} minutes old. As the agents that run on the account, it counts those
that ran at the reading, at their minutes then, each agent that started on the account since then, and each agent that
--start-on named the account for since then. So one pass of the loop starts only as many agents on each account as fit.
It adds a line with the type and the account that it named to .loop/meter.jsonl. For a builder type, it prints "no
account" once the agents that build at the newest reading, with each that started and each builder that it named an
account for since then, reach the limit that the last line names, and says why on standard error.

With --hand-off ID, the meter reads no account, and prints the note that scripts/hooks/hand-off gives an agent for its
account's room: soon, to hand off its work at its next stopping point, now, to hand off as soon as the step it's on
ends, or none. ID is the id of the agent's start line in .loop/agents.jsonl. The meter takes the newest reading of the
agent's account in .loop/meter.jsonl, unless it is more than {gap} minutes old, and counts the agents that run on the
account as --start-on does:

- Every agent on the account gets now once a meter's room is down to what the agents need to hand off: the usage of
  each one, and of the orchestrator at its share, for the time of a long hand-off. That time is the 90th percentile of
  the minutes from each agent's latest note of scripts/hooks/hand-off to its end line, or {hand_off} minutes until an
  agent with a note has ended.
- Before then, the agents that still run get soon, one at a time, the one that still needs the most first, until the
  room covers what the rest still need. So the agents closest to done finish on the account. An agent that got a note
  of the hook, about its context or its account's room, needs only the time of a long hand-off.

The line before the last lists the agents that run: those with a start line and no end line in .loop/agents.jsonl,
each with its type and the number of its issue or pull request. It leaves out an agent whose Claude Code process or
run of scripts/loop/run-builder has ended, such as a builder of an orchestrator that was restarted. It leaves out an
agent that the user interrupted too, until the agent gets a message, because Claude Code then runs no hook that writes
an end line. It gives each agent's context size, the threshold at which scripts/hooks/hand-off told the agent to hand
off its work, if it did, and whether the hook told it to hand off for its account's room.

The last line gives the machine's free disk, free memory, swap in use, GPU use and GPU memory in use, its load, the
orchestrator's context size, and whether a retrospective is due. One is due once the output tokens of the agents that
started since the last retrospective, reasoning tokens included, reach OUTPUT_TOKENS_PER_RETROSPECTIVE in this
script. The last retrospective is the newest merged pull request with the label retrospective, or the start of
.loop/agents.jsonl before there is one. The meter counts the output tokens as scripts/loop/report counts them, and an
agent of a session without an end line until the last line of its transcript. A request with no event in
.loop/requests.jsonl counts the output tokens that the transcript gives, which leave out most of them, so the line
says how many requests have none. Until a retrospective sets OUTPUT_TOKENS_PER_RETROSPECTIVE, one is due once the
agents have worked {hours} hours since the last retrospective instead. The hours come from the periods on each agent's
last end line in .loop/agents.jsonl, which scripts/loop/agent_log.py describes. An agent without an end line counts
until the last line of its transcript. The test builds and the auditors don't count.

A retrospective is due too when one of Claude Code's aliases opus, sonnet, haiku and fable names a model ID that
.claude/tested-models.json doesn't list. At most once a day, the meter sends one short request for each alias, on the
first Claude account, and reads the IDs from the result. It keeps them in .loop/models.json. The last line has a part
that starts with "retrospective due:" for each such ID, and one that starts with "model of" for each alias whose
request failed.

Once {building} agents that build run, the last line says so after the load, so that the loop starts no builder and no
test build until fewer run. The agents that build are those of a builder type, the Codex runs and the test builds of a
retrospective.

The last line ends with a part that starts with "audit due:" for each sign of clear waste, so that the orchestrator
starts a focused audit of it. scripts/loop/waste.py gives the thresholds of the three signs: a build of a builder type
that took far longer than the median of its type, heavy commands that waited long in scripts/loop/run-heavy's line over
the last hour, and passing runs of scripts/check that grew slower since the last retrospective. The meter writes the
subjects of the signs that it printed to .loop/printed-findings.json.

With --wake, the meter keeps reading, and prints and ends when an account's advice changes, when an account loses the
reading that it had at the start or gets back the one that it didn't have, when a reading has a sign of waste whose
subject the meter didn't print last time, when scripts/hooks/hand-off tells an agent to hand off its work, and 50
minutes after it started while an account's advice is stop. While an account has no reading, it reads at least every
{retry} minutes. Run it in the background, so that its end wakes the orchestrator.

.loop/accounts.json in the main checkout lists the accounts, each with the environment variables that select it:

  {"accounts": [{"name": "claude", "tool": "claude"},
                {"name": "codex", "tool": "codex", "env": {"CODEX_HOME": "~/.codex"}}]}

Without the file, the meter reads the Claude account that this machine is signed in to. It adds each reading of an
account to .loop/meter.jsonl, with the usage per agent-minute that it measured under "per_agent_minute", and the share
of its time that the orchestrator worked so far under "orchestrator_share", which is 0 on any account other than the
orchestrator's own. Each time it reads the accounts, it first adds a line with no account and the machine's numbers
under "machine", as scripts/loop/machine.py describes them. A number that the machine doesn't give is null. The meter
exits with code 1 when an account gives no reading. It reads the orchestrator's context size and the times when it
worked from the transcript of the session that the session: line of .loop/state.md names.
"""

